{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>saying: et my two sons, etc.</b> The reason he said <b>leimor</b> in this place is to say that he did not bring forth this matter from his mouth explicitly, because of the curse of a sage (Makkot 11a), but rather he spoke a statement whose understood meaning is this, yet he did not explicitly utter the death of his sons." "And his saying <b>et my two</b> means: two of those who exist, for he had four." "And his saying <b>tamit</b> means: you shall put me to death. And we find a punishment such as this, as they said, of blessed memory (Vayikra Rabbah 7:1), regarding the verse ״וּבְאַהֲרֹן הִתְאַנַּף״ etc. (Deuteronomy 9:20). And the reason Reuben did not wish to obligate himself except with two sons is that this is a severe punishment, for he would be lacking the commandment of procreation. Therefore he bound himself only with something that would not harm him in the World to Come. And for this reason Judah acted wisely when he saw that his father was not persuaded by Reuben's words, and he said (Genesis 43:9) ״וְחָטָאתִי לְאָבִי כָּל הַיָּמִים״ — he endangered himself also in the upper world." "<b>Put him on my hand.</b> Perhaps, for he was confident that he would be found clean and free of sin concerning Joseph, since nothing unseemly would befall him — therefore he was precise in saying <b>Put him on my hand</b> and I will return him, which is not so for the rest of the brothers, for they are guilty in the sale of Joseph."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:13.078030571Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}